{"question": "A 21-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his mother after being found lying unconscious next to a bottle of pills. The patient\u2019s mother mentions that he has been diagnosed with major depressive disorder 3 years ago for which he was taking a medication that resulted in only a slight improvement in his symptoms. She says he still found it difficult to concentrate on his studies and did not participate in any social events in college. He didn\u2019t have many friends and was often found sitting alone in his room. He has also threatened to take his life on several occasions, but she did not think he was serious. While recording his vital signs, the patient goes into a coma. His ECG shows a QT interval of 450 milliseconds. Which of the following medications did this patient most likely overdose on?", "options": {"A": "Bupropion", "B": "Sertraline", "C": "Venlafaxine", "D": "Citalopram", "E": "Clomipramine"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Psychiatry", "Emergency Medicine", "Toxicology", "Cardiology", "Pharmacology"], "option_domains": ["Psychiatry", "Toxicology"], "question_analyses": {"Psychiatry": "In this scenario, we have a 21-year-old man with a history of major depressive disorder who has attempted suicide by overdosing on pills. The key points to consider in this case are:\n\n1. **History of Major Depressive Disorder**: The patient has been diagnosed with major depressive disorder for three years, indicating a chronic condition that has not responded well to treatment. His symptoms include difficulty concentrating, social withdrawal, and a lack of friends, which are common in depression. 2. **Suicidal Ideation**: The patient has threatened to take his life on several occasions, which is a significant red flag. Even if his mother did not take these threats seriously, they indicate a serious level of distress and a potential risk for suicide. 3. **Acute Presentation**: The patient is found unconsci